## This Week's Standouts in Food Recalls
This week's recall roster reads like a suspenseful novel, only we're all unwilling protagonists. With 43 recalls logged in June alone -- and Class I events leading at 173 incidents for the year -- the food industry ensures our vigilance won't wane.

### Repeat Offenders: Hall of Shame
Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC persist as top repeat offenders, each tallying 11 recalls this year. Not to be outdone, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company matches their Class I tenacity with 10 major recalls. These brands have certainly mastered the art of maintaining the spotlight, but not in ways they'd hope.

### Salmonella Leads the Charge
This week's Class I recalls were generously peppered with Salmonella concerns, notably from JCB Flavors, LLC. The affected products include White Cheddar Seasoning gift sets and Dill & Garlic Seasoning, both posing significant health risks to consumers.

### Foreign Objects and Mislabeling
Straus Family Creamery reported two Class II recalls for their organic ice creams due to potential metal contamination. Mislabeling incidents like Market of Choice's Caesar Salad and the mislabeled Za'atar spice further underscore industry oversights.

### The Year So Far
With 543 recalls this year -- a pace well above last year's figures -- the food industry still struggles to confront recurring issues. Repeat offenders tally at 86, showcasing persistent systemic flaws.

### Final Thoughts
Consumers deserve safer food, yet recalls remain a grim necessity. Let's hope the industry sharpens its standards before our pantries become perilous landscapes.
